hey everyone i'm john coogan and this is  the first in a series of videos about  fundraising  i thought it would be useful to share  some of my experiences because  as of september 2020 i've been involved  in 10 different financing rounds there's  always an element of luck involved in  fundraising  but there are a few patterns that should  make the whole process a lot less  stressful if you follow them  i'm going to start every video with this  same message the best way to raise money  is to build a great company full stop  you can be absolutely awful at  fundraising  and do everything wrong but if your  company is making its customers happy  growing the customer base significantly  every month and has a giant pool of  potential customers  you will be able to raise money with  ease so before you focus on optimizing  your fundraising process  make sure you have spent orders of  magnitude more time optimizing your core  business  in thinking about fundraising for your  startup the first question you should  always ask yourself is whether or not  you should raise money for your startup  it's very easy to look at funding  announcements for startups and conflate  fundraising success with actual success  although the name of the game in venture  capital is to correlate these two as  closely as possible  the majority of venture capital  investments fail to produce a positive  return  raising money for your company does not  make your company successful  rapid growth is what makes your company  successful rapidly growing companies can  often use outside money to grow faster  and because they are growing so quickly  the process  for raising capital is often very easy  there are plenty of good reasons to  not want to raise money the most obvious  reason is what you give up for that  money  shares in your company if you believe  that your company is already on track to  be worth  billions of dollars and that additional  cash in the bank won't help you get  there  there is little reason to give up 20 or  more of your company  dilution not only affects your financial  return it can have big implications on  control of your company  once the founders own less than half of  the controlling shares  big decisions at the company become much  more complex  the second reason to hold off on raising  money is that it can be a huge  distraction  you can quickly get sucked into calls  coffees and dinners that can take up  most of your workday  this inevitably takes you away from your  most important job  actually growing your company lastly if  you don't believe you can actually  convince  investors to give you money for your  business there's no reason to spend time  fundraising  you won't just waste your time you'll  also hurt your reputation  with that out of the way let's talk  about some of the reasons why you would  want to raise money  the best reason is always that you've  found something that's working and  having extra cash in the bank will help  you scale the business faster  selling shares at an early stage will  dilute the amount that you own  so it better be worth it you need to be  crystal clear on where you will invest  the money within your business once you  have it to maximize growth  another reason lots of companies raise  money is that they simply cannot grow  off of profits alone  it's never good to be in a position  where you need to raise money  because you'll be on your back foot in  most negotiations but it does happen  in this case you should always look at  your business and see if there is a way  to restructure to get to breakeven  so you don't wind up raising money on  onerous terms  sometimes founders feel pressure to  raise money because their competitors  are raising money  this is completely natural since there  is a real threat in some industries  that the most well financed company will  win  the feeling of being second or third  behind a hot competitor with lots of  funding can be disappointing  but highly capitalized companies often  flame out so try not to let it get to  you  there are also other more rare  situations where fundraising can fulfill  a tangential goal  like forming a strategic partnership  with a large incumbent that could lead  to an acquisition  or providing liquidity to founders and  early employees but these funding rounds  usually only happen at the very late  stages of a company  so i'll focus mostly on examples where  your company is growing rapidly and  wants to expand  so once you've decided that raising  money is right for you how do you decide  what your funding target should be  again the best methodology is going to  be based on growth  how much money do you need to grow  aggressively and hit a new major  milestone for the company  you might look at what it will cost to  develop a new product or how much a  sales and marketing push will cost  ideally money invested in user  acquisition will turn into profits  quickly enough  that you won't need to raise a huge  round of financing to hit your growth  targets  but if your customer typically generates  profit for you over a long period of  time  this can be totally worth it internally  you might be looking at your current  burn rate and plan on raising enough  money to cover another year or two of  expenses  that can be a useful metric for managing  the business but it won't get investors  excited  no one wants to invest in a company that  plans on stagnating between rounds  so focus on growth other considerations  that influence how much you raise could  be tied to capital expenditures  r d costs or other industry specific  investments that you plan to make  these are all edge cases that depend on  the type of company you're building  lastly it's good to keep benchmarks in  mind since most investors will have  those in the back of their heads at all  times it's common for most valuations to  be between  five and ten times the amount of money  invested which is why twenty percent  dilution  tends to be a common benchmark for  founders this also varies widely based  on the growth trajectory of the company  and there are always exceptions once  you've decided that it's a good time to  raise money for your startup and you  know what your fundraising target is  the next phase is to figure out who to  raise money from so that will be the  subject of my next video  please hit the subscribe button and  click the bell if you want to be  notified when that video goes live  and if you have any questions or follow  ups that you'd like me to address  please leave me a comment below i try to  reply to every question that i receive  thanks for watching
 

